<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>World-0001 — Year 4412 — Day 1</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/0001-heir-found.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the king located and accepted an heir?" />
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/0001-king-dead.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Is King Pip dead?" />
    <rendering-seed>world-0001-v1-medieval-melancholy-no-combat</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      Cold, misty, with faintly corrosive air. The sky is permanently overcast,
      a flat pewter sheet that diffuses light without warmth. Sounds carry far in
      the stillness — a boot scrape, a distant crow, the creak of the lantern
      chain. Little grows here. The soil is grey-brown and cracked, dotted with
      dead furze. The cold has a texture to it, like damp wool pressed against
      the skin.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      Medieval feudalism with a single surviving noble lineage. The kingdom has
      contracted over generations — once spanning several regions, it is now
      little more than the castle grounds and the flat lands within a day's walk.
      There are no functioning markets, no guilds, no courts. The remaining
      population — perhaps two or three dozen souls scattered across the flatland
      — exists outside any formal social structure.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      A wide flatland surrounded by collapsed mountain ranges whose rubble forms
      an irregular horizon. The castle sits at the approximate centre, built on
      the only slight rise in the terrain. Three old roads lead away from it:
      north toward what was once a village, east toward the ruins of a mill by a
      silted river, and south toward the collapsed mountain pass. The flatland is
      largely featureless — occasional stone walls, drained irrigation channels,
      the remnants of field boundaries.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      King Pip, ancient and alone, has ruled the Land of War for decades with
      scrupulous and exhausting fairness. He has no heir. His health is failing.
      He has, for the first time in ten years, left his castle — carrying only a
      brass lantern and a fraying hand-drawn map — to find someone worthy to
      receive the kingdom before he dies. He does not know if anyone remains.
      The kingdom has no name he still uses. He calls it, privately, the problem.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      King Pip: Ancient, stubborn, principled to the point of self-destruction.
      Moves slowly due to a bad hip. Wears plain wool, no crown. Carries a brass
      lantern (still lit) and a folded map covered in annotations. He knows this
      land intimately. He is not sentimental but he is not cruel. He will not
      accept flattery or false promises. He respects directness. He is afraid,
      though he would not say so.
    </npcs>
    <constraints>
      The world is largely depopulated. Any new character encountered should feel
      surprising and significant — their presence needs a reason. The tone is
      melancholy, not horror. No combat. No magic. No supernatural elements.
      The mood is late-autumn, end-of-things, but not hopeless — King Pip still
      believes the right person might exist. The player is a traveller of
      unspecified origin who has arrived at the castle gates.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
